1.  What is NAT? (Network Address Translation)
    NAT lets many devices on a private network share a single public IP. Your router does this — all your home devices 
    have private IPs (192.168.x.x) but share one public IP. When you send a request, the router replaces your private IP with its public IP,
    and remembers to route the reply back to you. Solves IPv4 address exhaustion.

2.  What is subnetting and CIDR?
    Subnetting = dividing a large network into smaller sub-networks for better organization and security.
    CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) = the notation for this. 192.168.1.0/24 means the first 24 bits are the network part,
    leaving 8 bits for hosts (256 addresses, 254 usable). Common in cloud (AWS VPCs, Azure VNets) so very likely to come up.

3.  TCP 3-way handshake — how connections are established
    Step 1: Client sends SYN ("I want to connect, here's my sequence number")
    Step 2: Server replies with SYN-ACK ("Got it, here's mine, I acknowledge yours")
    Step 3: Client sends ACK ("Got yours, connection is open")
    After this, data flows. Connection is closed with a FIN-ACK sequence. This is fundamental — interviewers love this.

4.  What happens when you type a URL in a browser?
    This is a classic interview question. Full flow:
    1. Browser checks its DNS cache → if miss, asks DNS resolver → gets IP
    2. TCP 3-way handshake with server
    3. If HTTPS, TLS handshake (exchange certificates, agree on encryption)
    4. Browser sends HTTP GET request
    5. Server sends back HTTP response with HTML
    6. Browser parses HTML, fetches CSS/JS, renders page
    Know this end to end — it covers DNS, TCP, HTTP, TLS all in one.

5.  What is load balancing?
    Distributes incoming traffic across multiple servers so no single server is overwhelmed. 
    Common algorithms: Round Robin (rotate through servers), Least Connections (send to least busy), 
    IP Hash (same client always goes to same server). Load balancers can be hardware or software (Nginx, HAProxy). Critical for scalability 

6.  What is CDN? (Content Delivery Network)
    A network of geographically distributed servers that cache content closer to users.
    When you visit a website, you get content from the nearest CDN node, not the origin server in one location.
    Reduces latency, improves speed, reduces load on origin. Cloudflare, Akamai, AWS CloudFront are popular CDNs. 

7.  IPv4 vs IPv6
    IPv4: 32-bit addresses (~4.3 billion addresses). Running out due to internet growth. Format: 192.168.1.1
    IPv6: 128-bit addresses (~340 undecillion). Solves address exhaustion. Format: 2001:0db8:85a3::8a2e:0370:7334
    IPv6 also has built-in IPSec, better multicast, no need for NAT. Adoption is growing but IPv4 still dominates with NAT as a workaround.

8.  What is ARP? (Address Resolution Protocol)
    Translates IP addresses → MAC addresses within a local network. When device A wants to talk to device B (same network), 
    it broadcasts "Who has IP 192.168.1.5?" — device B replies with its MAC address. A caches this in its ARP table. 
    Works at Layer 2/3 boundary. 
